al source Third-party estimates, as reported by fool.com ; from gnews:37d2648cf990f4943cad6208 2026-07-14] Third-party estimates put NVIDIA’s share of the AI chip market at 80%.\\n    Quote: The good news is that Nvidia still controls an impressive 80% of the AI chip market, according to third-party estimates.\",\n   \"- e428 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Marvell Technology ; from gnews:37d2648cf990f4943cad6208 2026-07-14] Marvell says it designs custom AI chips for the top four U.S. hyperscalers and is expanding relationships with emerging hyperscalers.\\n    Quote: The company designs custom AI chips for Amazon , Microsoft , and others. Marvell claims it designs chips for the top four U.S. hyperscalers and is also expanding its relationships with emerging hyperscalers.\",\n   \"- e429 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Marvell Technology ; from gnews:37d2648cf990f4943cad6208 2026-07-14] Marvell said it could pursue more than 50 custom AI chip opportunities across over 10 customers, with potential lifetime revenue of $75 billion.\\n    Quote: In all, Marvell notes that it can tap into more than 50 custom AI chip opportunities across over 10 customers in the long run, leading to potential lifetime revenue of $75 billion.\",\n   \"- e430 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:37d2648cf990f4943cad6208 2026-07-14] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang called Marvell a potential next trillion-dollar company; the article said Marvell’s market capitalization was $195 billion.\\n    Quote: Just last month, Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang touted Marvell as the next trillion-dollar company. It currently has a market cap of $195 billion.\",\n   \"- e431 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:37d2648cf990f4943cad6208 2026-07-14] Jensen Huang said Marvell’s networking chips would play a critical role in AI data centers by enabling AI tasks to run seamlessly.\\n    Quote: Huang pointed out that Marvell's networking chips will play a critical role in AI data centers, enabling AI tasks to run seamlessly.\",\n   \"- e432 [rumour; Tom's Hardware, relaying a Financial Times report based on unnamed sources; original source NVIDIA, as reported by unnamed sources to the Financial Times ; from gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reportedly cut its authorized-customer roster in Asia by more than half and tightened verification, including customer-site visits, contract checks and end-user interviews, to reduce chip smuggling into China.\\n    Quote: According to the Financial Times , this roster cuts the number of authorized clients by more than half, with those remaining having passed tougher compliance inspections to ensure that they are genuine businesses, not shell companies designed to forward Nvidia GPUs and servers into China. Some of the steps that Nvidia took to help safeguard its chips reportedly included sending staff to customer d\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e475, e478; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e433 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source The Chinese government ; from gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb 2026-07-14] Beijing reportedly continued to bar Chinese companies from buying NVIDIA H200 GPUs, relying instead on domestic semiconductor suppliers to address the shortfall.\\n    Quote: However, Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy these AI processors — instead, it’s banking on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to make up for the shortfall, but it’s apparently still not enough.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e203 (2026-07-08); both are in force: Chinese officials reportedly told Alibaba, ByteDance and DeepSeek they would be permitted to purchase some Nvidia H200 chips; the report was published July 8, 2026.\",\n   \"- e434 [third_party_view; Tom's Hardware, relaying an unnamed technology executiv